WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.100
Thanks for tuning into SpaceX Daily Episode 27.

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:04.860
I'm Patrick in Vancouver.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.440
Today is July 9th, 2026.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.420
Let's get into today's SpaceX developments.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:15.140
Falcon 9's 36th flight today extends booster life limits

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:19.020
and sustains the cadence needed for Starlink's record deployment year.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:24.640
Booster B1067 lifted off from Cape Canaveral Space Force Station

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:31.080
pad 40 at 5.25am Eastern Daylight Time on the Starlink 10-42 mission

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.840
carrying nearly 30 satellites.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:38.840
This marks the booster's 36th flight and surpasses all prior Falcon 9 reuse counts.

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:43.540
The launch continues the programs pushed toward higher annual flight rates

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:46.320
while testing the practical ceiling on booster life.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:51.360
This development fits the ongoing Falcon launch cadence and reuse arc

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:55.320
by directly probing the key open question of booster life limits.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:01:00.020
Observers will watch post-flight inspections for any signs of accumulating

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:02.560
wear on tanks, engines, or grid fins.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:07.400
Multiple Starlink missions this week, including the 10-42 flight,

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:11.440
keep the constellation on pace for a new annual deployment high.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:15.500
The current cadence relies on rapid Falcon 9 turnarounds

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:19.820
and the PES dispenser architecture that releases satellites in quick succession.

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:25.680
This volume growth directly supports the subscriber and direct-to-cell expansion goals

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:31.720
tracked in prior episodes. It advances the Starlink program's tracked open questions around

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:34.880
subscriber growth and direct-to-cell capability ramp.

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:40.840
Next milestones include confirmation of successful orbit raising and initial laser link

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:46.880
activation across the new batch. The Starship PES dispenser now allows satellite operators

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:52.120
to design around high volume low-cost rides rather than dictating vehicle requirements.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:57.680
This inversion reduces the historical pressure on rocket developers to customize for

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.800
each payload's mass or orbit needs. The design choice lowers per satellite integration time

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:09.940
and supports the economics of large constellations. Watch for how future Starlink batches or

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:14.300
external customers adapt their hardware to exploit the new dispenser geometry.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:20.880
Notices to Airmen covering Mexican airspace have been released for a potential Starship test window

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:28.640
opening July 15 at 5.45 pm central daylight time. The advisories mark the next step in preparing

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:34.420
for the upcoming integrated flight test. This follows the ongoing campaign at Starbase where

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:40.420
booster catch hardware and ship heat shield iterations remain the focus. It builds on the Starship

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:46.640
program last covered on July 8 by advancing the open questions of full and rapid reuse cadence

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:53.380
and orbital refueling demonstration timeline. Listeners should monitor subsequent FAA and range

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:59.440
updates for any schedule adjustments. The early morning ascent created a visible jellyfish-shaped

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.480
exhaust pattern caused by sunlight illuminating the expanding plume at high altitude.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:10.720
The effect is a known optical result of the Falcon 9's Merlin exhaust interacting with the

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:16.880
upper atmosphere under specific lighting conditions. It does not affect vehicle performance or payload

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:22.420
delivery. Similar visuals have accompanied prior dawn or dusk launches from the cape.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:28.680
The science involves the interaction of the exhaust plume with atmospheric layers at altitude

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:35.000
during the ascent profile. Ground teams reported nominal performance through stage separation and

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:40.480
second engine start on the record flight. The second stage completed its burns without anomaly

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:46.400
before releasing the satellites. The payload count aligns with the current standard manifest for

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:52.840
Falcon 9 Starlink flights. Deployment occurred after the second stage burn placed the satellites in

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:58.760
their initial parking orbit. On-orbit checks will confirm solar array deployment and propulsion

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:04.860
system activation. The satellites will then begin their climb to operational altitude using onboard

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:12.820
ion thrusters. Booster B1067 completed its 36th mission extending the demonstrated reuse envelope

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:19.140
for the Merlin-powered first stage. The flight profile remained within the parameters established by

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:25.440
earlier high flight count boosters. Engineers will review telemetry for any shifts in engine performance

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:31.800
or structural margins. The booster returned to a drone ship landing after separation from the second

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:38.840
stage. Observers on R-slash SpaceX noted the rapid turnaround between recent Florida launches and

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:43.960
questioned whether pad 40 maintenance windows will stay short enough to support the current manifest.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:50.360
Amateur photographers shared high-resolution images of the jellyfish plume and discussed the exact

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:56.540
altitude and sun angle geometry required to produce the shape. Users tracking starship test

00:04:56.540 --> 00:05:02.100
infrastructure pointed out that the new Mexican no-tams cover a wider corridor than flight 12,

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:08.840
suggesting possible changes in the expected trajectory or landing zone. Several accounts highlighted

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:14.560
the R's Technica piece on the PES dispenser and debated how ride share customers might redesign

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:20.380
their satellites to take fuller advantage of the volume now available. Worth keeping an eye on,

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:26.640
early no-tam coverage for Starship Flight 13 still leaves the actual launch date subject to final

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:33.740
FAA and range approvals. Any extension of the review process would push the test beyond the current mid-July

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:39.380
window and delay data collection on the latest booster catch hardware and heat shield tiles.

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:45.660
Resolution depends on timely submission of updated safety documentation and weather windows at

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:53.720
Starbase. On the AI front, UBS analysts argue that Starship's eventual high mass to orbit capability

00:05:53.720 --> 00:06:00.340
could let SpaceX place large-scale compute hardware directly in orbit, creating a new revenue

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:06.680
stream tied to AI infrastructure demand. The note links this potential to StarLinks existing laser

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:12.860
link network for data movement between orbital nodes and ground stations. No specific hardware

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:18.300
quantities or timelines are provided in the coverage. From an engineering standpoint, the 36

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:26.240
flight of booster B1067 offers a clear window into the physics of reuse limits. Each prior mission

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:32.600
leaves residual thermal and mechanical stress in the tank walls, engine mounts and interstage structure.

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:38.360
The cumulative effect is governed by fatigue crack growth rates under repeated pressure cycles

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:45.020
and vibration spectra. By flying the same hardware this many times, SpaceX is effectively measuring

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:50.780
how far the original factor of safety margins can be consumed before inspection or refurbishment

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:57.460
becomes mandatory. The practical payoff appears in propellant and labor costs per kilogram delivered.

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:03.760
If the raw material and energy cost of building one Falcon 9 first stage is treated as the floor,

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:09.980
each additional flight divides that fixed cost across more missions. Driving the idiot index,

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:16.100
the ratio of delivered launch price to raw material cost steadily downward. The current record

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:21.620
demonstrates that the index has already dropped far enough to make high cadence Starlink replenishment

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:28.560
economically viable, without new booster production at the same rate. Looking ahead, the open variable

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:34.060
is whether the same margins hold once the vehicle begins carrying the heavier payloads or more

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:41.460
aggressive trajectories that Starship operations will eventually demand. Continued flights of B1067

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:47.340
and its peers will reveal whether engine bay thermal protection or grid fin actuator life becomes

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:53.520
the binding constraint before the tanks themselves. Data from the post flight inspection will therefore

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:59.660
feed directly into decisions on whether to extend the reuse target beyond 40 flights or introduce

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:04.780
targeted design changes for the next block of boosters. And a quick market note,

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:15.800
SPCX is at $148.30, down 0.4% versus the previous close. Before we go, post flight inspection

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:24.120
results on B1067 and any follow up FAA updates on the July 15th Starship window will be the next

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:32.060
concrete signals to track. That covers everything worth knowing about SpaceX today. SPCX is trading at

00:08:32.060 --> 00:08:41.340
$148.30, down 0.4%. A quick rating on Apple podcasts or Spotify goes a long way.

